### Authentication OTP API Documentation
### Complete API documentation for OTP (One-Time Password) authentication endpoints

@baseUrl = http://localhost:5000/api
@contentType = application/json

### ============================================
### SEND OTP
### ============================================
### Send a 6-digit OTP to the user's phone number
### The user must already exist in the system (created during registration)
### No authentication required

POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/send-otp
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "phone": "+1234567890"
}

### Response Example (Success):
### {
###   "success": true,
###   "message": "OTP sent successfully",
###   "data": {
###     "message": "OTP sent successfully",
###     "otpToken": "jwt-otp-token-here",
###     "otp": "123456"
###   }
### }

### Response Example (User Not Found):
### {
###   "success": false,
###   "message": "User not found"
### }

### Notes:
### - OTP is a 6-digit random number (100000-999999)
### - OTP is stored in the user record
### - An otpToken is returned which can be used for verification
### - The OTP in the response is for testing/development purposes
### - In production, OTP should be sent via SMS/email service

### ============================================
### VERIFY OTP
### ============================================
### Verify the OTP and authenticate the user
### Returns JWT access token and refresh token upon successful verification
### No authentication required

POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/verify-otp
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "phone": "+1234567890",
  "otp": "123456",
  "fcmToken": "optional-fcm-token-for-push-notifications"
}

### Verify OTP without FCM Token:
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/verify-otp
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "phone": "+1234567890",
  "otp": "123456"
}

### Response Example (Success):
### {
###   "success": true,
###   "message": "OTP verified successfully",
###   "data": {
###     "token": "jwt-access-token-here",
###     "refreshToken": "jwt-refresh-token-here",
###     "tokenVersion": 1
###   }
### }

### Response Example (Invalid OTP):
### {
###   "success": false,
###   "message": "Invalid OTP"
### }

### Response Example (User Not Found):
### {
###   "success": false,
###   "message": "User not found"
### }

### ============================================
### COMPLETE AUTHENTICATION FLOW
### ============================================

### Step 1: Send OTP
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/send-otp
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "phone": "+1234567890"
}

### Step 2: Verify OTP (use the OTP from Step 1 response or SMS)
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/verify-otp
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "phone": "+1234567890",
  "otp": "123456",
  "fcmToken": "fcm-token-for-push-notifications"
}

### Step 3: Use the access token for authenticated requests
### Example: Get user's projects
GET {{baseUrl}}/projects/my-projects
Content-Type: {{contentType}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### ============================================
### COMPREHENSIVE NOTES & GUIDELINES
### ============================================

### 1. AUTHENTICATION FLOW
###    - Step 1: User requests OTP by providing phone number
###    - Step 2: System generates 6-digit OTP and stores it
###    - Step 3: OTP is sent to user (via SMS/email in production)
###    - Step 4: User submits OTP for verification
###    - Step 5: System verifies OTP and issues JWT tokens
###    - Step 6: User uses access token for authenticated requests

### 2. PHONE NUMBER FORMAT
###    - Phone number must be a string
###    - Minimum length: 1 character
###    - Maximum length: 15 characters
###    - Format: Can include country code (e.g., "+1234567890")
###    - Phone number must match an existing user in the system

### 3. OTP FORMAT
###    - OTP must be exactly 6 digits
###    - OTP must contain only numbers (0-9)
###    - OTP is case-sensitive (must match exactly)
###    - OTP is generated randomly between 100000 and 999999
###    - OTP is stored in the user record until verified or expired

### 4. OTP TOKEN
###    - otpToken is a JWT token containing OTP information
###    - Used internally for OTP verification process
###    - Has a limited expiration time
###    - Not the same as the access token

### 5. FCM TOKEN (OPTIONAL)
###    - Firebase Cloud Messaging token for push notifications
###    - Optional field in verify-otp request
###    - If provided, it's stored in user's fcmTokens array
###    - Used to send push notifications to the user's device
###    - Multiple FCM tokens can be stored per user (multiple devices)
###    - Duplicate tokens are automatically filtered

### 6. ACCESS TOKEN
###    - JWT token containing user information
###    - Used for authenticated API requests
###    - Include in Authorization header: Bearer <token>
###    - Contains user ID, phone, verification status, mode, etc.
###    - Has expiration time (typically 1 day)
###    - Token version is included to support token invalidation

### 7. REFRESH TOKEN
###    - JWT token used to obtain new access tokens
###    - Stored in user record
###    - Expires after 7 days
###    - Used when access token expires
###    - Token version is included for security

### 8. TOKEN VERSION
###    - Incremented on each OTP verification
###    - Used to invalidate all previous tokens (single device login)
###    - Prevents use of old tokens after new login
###    - Included in both access and refresh tokens

### 9. USER VERIFICATION
###    - phoneVerification is set to true after successful OTP verification
###    - OTP is cleared from user record after verification
###    - User must be active (isDeleted: false) to receive OTP
###    - User must exist in the system before sending OTP

### 10. ERROR HANDLING
###     - 200 OK: OTP sent successfully
###     - 200 OK: OTP verified successfully
###     - 400 Bad Request: Invalid OTP format or invalid OTP
###     - 404 Not Found: User not found
###     - 500 Internal Server Error: Server error

### 11. SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
###     - OTP is single-use (cleared after verification)
###     - OTP should be sent via secure channel (SMS/email)
###     - OTP has expiration time (should be implemented in SMS service)
###     - Token version prevents token reuse after logout/login
###     - Phone number must match existing user (prevents enumeration)

### 12. TESTING & DEVELOPMENT
###     - In development, OTP is returned in the response for testing
###     - In production, OTP should only be sent via SMS/email
###     - Use test phone numbers for development
###     - OTP format: 6 digits, numeric only

### 13. REQUEST VALIDATION
###     Send OTP:
###     - phone: Required, string, 1-15 characters
###     
###     Verify OTP:
###     - phone: Required, string, 1-15 characters
###     - otp: Required, string, exactly 6 digits, numeric only
###     - fcmToken: Optional, string

### 14. RESPONSE STRUCTURE
###     Send OTP Response:
###     {
###       "success": boolean,
###       "message": string,
###       "data": {
###         "message": string,
###         "otpToken": string (JWT),
###         "otp": string (6 digits) - for testing only
###       }
###     }
###     
###     Verify OTP Response:
###     {
###       "success": boolean,
###       "message": string,
###       "data": {
###         "token": string (JWT access token),
###         "refreshToken": string (JWT refresh token),
###         "tokenVersion": number
###       }
###     }

### 15. USAGE EXAMPLES

### Example 1: Basic Authentication Flow
### Step 1: Send OTP
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/send-otp
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "phone": "+966501234567"
}

### Step 2: Verify OTP (use OTP from SMS or response)
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/verify-otp
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "phone": "+966501234567",
  "otp": "123456"
}

### Step 3: Use Access Token
GET {{baseUrl}}/projects/my-projects
Authorization: Bearer <token-from-step-2>

### Example 2: Authentication with Push Notifications
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/send-otp
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "phone": "+966501234567"
}

POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/verify-otp
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "phone": "+966501234567",
  "otp": "123456",
  "fcmToken": "dGhpcyBpcyBhIGZha2UgZmNtIHRva2VuIGZvciB0ZXN0aW5n"
}

### Example 3: Error Scenarios

### Invalid Phone Number Format
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/send-otp
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "phone": ""
}

### Response: 400 Bad Request
### {
###   "success": false,
###   "message": "Phone is required"
### }

### User Not Found
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/send-otp
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "phone": "+999999999999"
}

### Response: 404 Not Found
### {
###   "success": false,
###   "message": "User not found"
### }

### Invalid OTP Format
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/verify-otp
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "phone": "+1234567890",
  "otp": "12345"
}

### Response: 400 Bad Request
### {
###   "success": false,
###   "message": "OTP must be exactly 6 digits"
### }

### Invalid OTP Value
POST {{baseUrl}}/auth/verify-otp
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "phone": "+1234567890",
  "otp": "000000"
}

### Response: 400 Bad Request
### {
###   "success": false,
###   "message": "Invalid OTP"
### }

### 16. INTEGRATION NOTES
###     - OTP endpoints are public (no authentication required)
###     - User must exist before sending OTP
###     - OTP is generated server-side (never trust client-generated OTP)
###     - Access token should be stored securely (e.g., secure HTTP-only cookie or secure storage)
###     - Refresh token should be stored securely and used to refresh access token
###     - Token version allows for logout-all-devices functionality

### 17. BEST PRACTICES
###     - Always validate phone number format on client side before sending
###     - Implement rate limiting to prevent OTP spam
###     - Use SMS service with expiration (e.g., 5-10 minutes)
###     - Clear OTP from response in production (only return in development)
###     - Implement OTP resend functionality with cooldown period
###     - Store tokens securely and implement token refresh mechanism
###     - Handle token expiration gracefully in the client

### 18. TOKEN USAGE
###     After successful OTP verification, use the access token:
###     
###     Authorization: Bearer <access-token>
###     
###     Example authenticated request:
###     GET {{baseUrl}}/projects/my-projects
###     Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9...
###     
###     When access token expires, use refresh token to get a new access token
###     (refresh token endpoint should be implemented separately)
